WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:06.260
From awesome Thena action, lost-in-time Captain
America shields, a deleted Batman parody,

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.960
and up to nine alternate endings, there's
a lot that Marvel deleted from the final version

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:11.960
of Eternals.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.950
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing all the cut scenes

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:20.070
and characters, alternate post-credit scenes,
and Deviant designs that ended up getting

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:21.070
the chop.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.500
Spoilers ahead, of course, so take care.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.630
There was a great looking scene with Thena
in the first trailer that didn't make it into

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:31.340
the movie which seems to be part of what Makkari
and Kingo's actors have described as a visually

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.670
spectacular deleted scene set during the flashback
to the Gupta empire.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:40.079
The scene involved a lot of silk and would
have established the two types of Eternals;

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:43.960
the thinkers, that's Sersi, Ajak, Phastos,
Sprite and Druig;

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.480
and the fighters, that's Ikaris, Gilgamesh,
Thena, Makkari, and Kingo.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:52.269
It was intended to show how the family were
both dysfunctional and fun at the same time,

00:00:52.269 --> 00:00:56.210
but was cut for pacing which, given the length
of the movie, makes sense.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:00.429
What's especially intriguing about this deleted
scene is that this guy here is carrying a

00:01:00.429 --> 00:01:04.699
shield very similar to Steve Roger's famous
Captain America shield.

00:01:04.699 --> 00:01:09.290
That's strange because chronologically in
the main MCU timeline, it was Howard Stark

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:14.320
who first created Cap's shield in the 20th
century, whereas the Gupta scenes in Eternals

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.940
take place in 400 AD, many hundreds of years
earlier.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:23.370
Steve Rogers did, of course, decide to go
time-travelling at the end of Avengers: Endgame

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:27.550
for his rendezvous with Peggy Carter, and
he also brought back a new Captain America

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:28.760
shield for Sam Wilson.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.700
So, did he also decide to go back further
in time and have something to do with the

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.830
shield we see in this scene?

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:39.490
Alternatively, and more likely, Phastos invented
this shield and kept it in storage on the

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:44.270
Eternals ship and released the idea for it
later on in time to Howard Stark, similar

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:49.520
to how he slowly introduces other technology
over the years to humankind in this film.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.470
A line widely used in the Eternals trailers
and TV spots but which didn't make it into

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:57.160
the final movie is when Ikaris says…

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.160
Eternals assemble.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:59.250
It's a really good line.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:03.570
It's a nod, of course, to the comics and that
awesome moment when Cap finally said the famous

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:09.600
line in Endgame as he called for Earth's Mightiest
Heroes to wade into battle against Thanos.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.600
Avengers!

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:11.600
Assemble.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.420
Another deleted line from the trailers would
have come at the end of the movie when Sprite

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.400
is sitting with Ikaris watching the volcano
erupt as the Emergence begins.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.310
If this is what the end of the world looks
like, at least we have front row seats.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.270
A character I really enjoyed in the movie
was Kingo and the comedic moments that both

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.030
he and his human friend Karun brought to the
story.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:34.940
The documentary they begin filming on Kingo's
jet is hilarious, and reminds me of the Thor

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:37.740
mockumentaries;
and, in fact, there's an Avengers name-drop

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.500
in the trailers that was cut from the final
movie.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.500
Avengers..

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:42.500
Thor..

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:43.500
Spider-Man.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.020
Another deleted scene from Kingo's jet has
Sprite holding a microphone and performing

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:48.670
what looks like karaoke.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:52.550
The others behind her appear to be sleeping
and, given there's several cocktails on the

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:55.950
bar, it looks like this is some kind of party
scene on the jet.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:03:00.880
An area where I thought the movie did impress
was its visual style, especially the Deviants

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.280
which had some particularly striking looks.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.480
The scene where their leader Kro uses his
tentacles to transform the other Deviants

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:11.920
in the cave into monstrous winged and wolf-like
creatures was especially shocking.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.980
However, there were some even more horrifying
Deviant designs that didn't make it into the

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:16.980
film.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.770
Leaked concept art shows frog-like creatures
and scorpion-style monsters that weren't in

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:22.990
the final movie.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:27.140
The frog-like Deviant might have been based
on the Marvel character Brother Tode, who

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:32.660
in the comics was a ruler of the ancient Deviant
empire and also commanded the Deviant warlord

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:33.660
Kro.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.610
The name on the concept art for the scorpion
Deviant indicates the creature was based on

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:41.900
a mythological Mesopotamian half-human, half-scorpion
creature.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:46.040
I think this would have been an especially
interesting Deviant for the Babylonian attack,

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:49.550
given how these scorpion creatures appear
in the Epic of Gilgamesh.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:53.670
Interestingly, the real-world myths of the
Scorpion Men say they were created by the

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:58.750
ocean goddess Tiamat, whose name is reminiscent
of Tiamut, the Celestial whose birth in the

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:01.349
Indian Ocean is stopped by Sersi.

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:05.671
These other Deviant designs, which appear
to be by concept artist Tully Summers, seem

00:04:05.671 --> 00:04:09.610
to have inspired the look of some of the other
versions of the Deviants in the movie, such

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:15.040
as the winged monster, the wolf-like beast,
and the long-legged almost spider-like creatures.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.109
Some of the humanoid designs also appear to
be the inspiration for Kro's more human-like

00:04:19.109 --> 00:04:21.659
appearance that he acquires as the movie goes
on.

00:04:21.659 --> 00:04:26.760
As well as deleted antagonists, there were
also two Eternal characters cut from the final

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:27.760
film.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:31.889
Director Chloe Zhao revealed that there were
12 Eternals in the original script, but that

00:04:31.889 --> 00:04:33.939
two of them had to be "let go".

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:38.849
And co-screenwriters Kaz and Ryan Firpo elaborated
on Deadline's podcast saying that although

00:04:38.849 --> 00:04:43.949
Marvel initially asked for 12 characters,
after the first draft, they felt it was just

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:49.229
too many brand new characters to introduce
all at once to the MCU audience.

00:04:49.229 --> 00:04:53.879
One of those deleted characters was Vampiro,
a somewhat obscure Eternal, who despite his

00:04:53.879 --> 00:04:56.340
name and fangs, isn't a vampire.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:00.930
In fact, he's more of a Batman knock-off in
terms of his suit, look, and use of high-tech

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:02.110
gadgets.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:05.069
Given the numerous DC references already in
the movie…

00:05:05.069 --> 00:05:08.740
That's Superman with the cape and you're shooting
laser beams out of your eyes,

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:12.939
…a Batman-style character would certainly
have fit in, however, it's just as well the

00:05:12.939 --> 00:05:19.199
number of Eternals was cut down because even
with 10 the film did feel somewhat overstuffed.

00:05:19.199 --> 00:05:23.490
And thinking back to Guardians of the Galaxy,
for example, their team began with just five

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.190
new characters.

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:30.009
In true Marvel style, Eternals has a couple
of post-credits scenes setting up new storylines

00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:33.580
in the MCU;
however, screenwriter Kaz Firpo has revealed

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:37.580
that there were a total of nine post-credit
scenes originally considered for the film.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.249
In an interview with Metro, he explained that
each of the

00:05:40.249 --> 00:05:44.030
"possible versions of [the] ending told [a]
different story [with] different characters

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:47.759
[and] different ideas",
and he added that that some of them were "really

00:05:47.759 --> 00:05:48.759
spicy".

00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:52.659
Given what we know about Marvel's upcoming
movie slate, other possibilities for the end-credits

00:05:52.659 --> 00:05:56.199
scenes could have been Doctor Strange 2 or
Thor: Love and Thunder.

00:05:56.199 --> 00:05:58.699
But, what would you have liked to have seen?

00:05:58.699 --> 00:06:02.909
While discussing his stunt work and training,
Druig actor Barry Keoghan revealed that his

00:06:02.909 --> 00:06:06.699
mind-controlling character had at least one
fight scene cut from the film.

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:10.069
In an interview with Comic Book Movie, he
said that writer-director

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:14.639
"Chloe had written a boxing scene [and his
character] was avoiding punches and the tail,

00:06:14.639 --> 00:06:15.770
and stuff like that."

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:19.710
Which sounds like he might have had a fight
scene with either Ikaris and also perhaps

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:24.240
Kro, or possibly another Deviant, given he
says Druig was avoiding a tail.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.550
By the way, interestingly enough, actor Keoghan
is also a bit of an amateur boxer in real

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:28.550
life.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:33.600
Some other scenes cut from the film include
shots in the trailer of a longer opening scene,

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:38.969
Sersi passing her hand over crops in Babylon,
which has echoes of Thanos the farmer in Endgame

00:06:38.969 --> 00:06:40.150
tending to his crops.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.360
And Ajak saying
"Hell, yes"

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:46.189
during her reunion with Ikaris at the house
in South Dakota.

00:06:46.189 --> 00:06:49.881
And it wouldn't be a Marvel movie, without
some altered trailer scenes that ended up

00:06:49.881 --> 00:06:52.259
quite different in the final film.

00:06:52.259 --> 00:06:56.520
In the past, these have been done either to
avoid giving away spoilers or just filmed

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.680
exclusively for promotional purposes.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.680
So, in the first Eternals trailer when Thena
wields her sword at this moment her eyes are

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:07.830
normal, whereas in the movie this scene takes
place while she has the Mahd Wy'ry, and so

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:09.990
her eyes are actually white.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:14.169
When Sersi says "it's beautiful" to Ikaris
in the first trailer, it appears to happen

00:07:14.169 --> 00:07:19.309
as the Eternals are arriving on Earth, whereas
in the movie this moment happens just after

00:07:19.309 --> 00:07:23.770
the Eternals have been created and the view
outside the window is very different with

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:27.279
Sersi and Ikaris looking down on Earth from
space.

00:07:27.279 --> 00:07:31.349
And when Ikaris suggests he could lead the
Avengers now that Iron Man and Steve Rogers

00:07:31.349 --> 00:07:35.559
aren't around, the trailer has everyone bursting
into laughter.

00:07:35.559 --> 00:07:38.331
So now that Captain Rogers and Iron Man are
both gone, who do you think's going to lead

00:07:38.331 --> 00:07:39.649
the Avengers?

00:07:39.649 --> 00:07:42.909
I could lead them.

00:07:42.909 --> 00:07:46.289
[laughter]
In the movie, however, that reaction doesn't

00:07:46.289 --> 00:07:51.020
happen, and instead Kingo agrees with Ikaris
whereas Gilgamesh simply points out that Ajak

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:54.270
didn't even choose him to lead the Eternals.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:58.580
Another alternate trailer scene is a cool
close-up of Makkari speed-reading her way

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:03.020
through a book, whereas in the final movie
the shot is only seen from much further out

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:05.039
and doesn't quite have the same effect.

00:08:05.039 --> 00:08:09.169
So, do you wish any of these deleted scenes
had been included in the film?

00:08:09.169 --> 00:08:12.860
And how do you rate Eternals compared to your
favourite MCU movies?

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:14.520
Comment with your thoughts below.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:18.580
And if you enjoyed this video, a thumbs-up
and a share are hugely appreciated.

